{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310811,"nonce":"N2l2pwo5SkzMZHNZsmJuR9n6x0s=","title":"Persistent spin-wait worker pool shipped at commit 4001079. User-measured +28-35% on sustained decode (50 → 64-67 tok/s on 808-token ...","message":"User measurements on 808-token sustained decode (Voronoi-shape prompt):\n- Pre-c5ab136 baseline: ~40 tok/s\n- Post-c5ab136 (llama.cpp kernel port): ~50 tok/s\n- Post-4001079 with RAYZOR_KERNEL_TIMING=1 (per-call profile on): 64.4 - 67.6 tok/s\n- **Post-4001079 with timing OFF: \u003e70 tok/s steadily** (2026-06-08)\n\nVs llama.cpp on the same M1 Pro / Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M:\n- llama.cpp CPU: 87 tok/s\n- **rayzor at HEAD (timing off): ~80% of llama.cpp** (was 32% at session start, 58% after kernel port)\n\n\nThe 64-67 tok/s number that appears in the commit message for 4001079\nand the first revision of this memory was measured with\n`RAYZOR_KERNEL_TIMING=1` set, because the workflow that motivated the\nspin pool also produced per-call breakdowns. With timing on, every\ninstrumented FFI entry pays `Instant::now()` + `elapsed()` + atomic\nadds (~26 timers × hundreds of calls per token = a noticeable wall-time\ntax). Disabling timing (the production configuration) gives the \u003e70\ntok/s steady-state. Future perf measurements for production-comparable\nnumbers MUST run with timing off; cite the in-session number only\nwhen explicitly comparing per-call breakdowns.\n\nStacked session impact:\n- c5ab136 (kernel port): +15-25% (short-form +15%, long-form ~+25%)\n- 4001079 (spin pool):    +28-35% on top (only on sustained decode)\n- Combined: ~2.5x decode tok/s vs session start\n\n\nReplaces `mpsc::channel + condvar wake + queue mutex` with:\n- Per-worker `WorkerSlot` (state: AtomicU8, trampoline: AtomicPtr, closure_ptr: AtomicPtr, lo/hi: AtomicUsize)\n- All slots cache-line padded (128-byte align) so workers don't false-share\n- Global `pending: AtomicUsize` countdown\n- Type-erased trampoline: `fn(*const (), usize, usize)` cast from\n  `trampoline_for::\u003cF\u003e` per dispatch\n\nWorker loop:\n```rust\nloop {\n  loop {  // spin-wait\n    if slot.state.load(Acquire) == 1 { break; }\n    spin_count += 1;\n    if spin_count \u003e= 8192 { spin_count = 0; check_shutdown; }\n    std::hint::spin_loop();\n  }\n  let (lo, hi, t, c) = read Relaxed;\n  trampoline(c, lo, hi);\n  slot.state.store(0, Release);\n  pending.fetch_sub(1, Release);\n}\n```\n\nDispatcher:\n```rust\npending.store(n_dispatched, Release);\nfor w in 0..n_dispatched {\n  write Relaxed fields;\n  slot.state.store(1, Release);\n}\nwhile pending.load(Acquire) \u003e 0 { std::hint::spin_loop(); }\n```\n\n\nEarlier `bugs_forkjoin_atomic_countdown_no_win` (commit reverted) tried\nAtomicUsize+Condvar countdown — washed at sub-noise. Why?\n\nPre-c5ab136: per-worker matmul work was 187.5 µs. condvar wake ~5-15 µs\nwas sub-noise (3-8% of per-worker). Replacing it saved nothing visible.\n\nPost-c5ab136: per-worker matmul work is 70.9 µs. condvar wake at 5-15 µs\nis now 7-21% of per-worker time. Spin-wait wakes in ~50-100 ns —\ntwo orders of magnitude faster. The kernel port made this lever visible.\n\nLesson: micro-overhead optimizations have visibility thresholds. The\nsame code change can wash on heavy inner work and win on light inner\nwork. Always re-measure after a kernel-level improvement before\ndiscarding \"previously washed\" optimizations.\n\n\n80-token decode shows higher variance with spin pool (single A/B run\nshowed 4.6-28.4 tok/s vs legacy stable 26-29 tok/s). The 80-token\nregime has fewer fork-joins, so OS scheduling effects don't amortise\nacross many calls and short bursts of background contention hurt\nproportionally more.\n\nSustained inference (300+ tokens, real chat/agent workloads) benefits\nunambiguously because thousands of fork-joins amortise the per-call\nspin wins.\n\nWorkaround: set `RAYZOR_LEGACY_POOL=1` for short-prompt benchmarks or\npower-sensitive workloads where the spin pool's idle-time CPU is\nunacceptable.\n\n\nUser-measured peak RSS on 808-token sustained Voronoi decode at HEAD:\n**690 MB**. Below the prior flash-attn-fix observation of ~850 MB and\nbelow the llama.cpp ~1-1.5 GB footprint class for the same model+ctx.\n\nReplacing `mpsc::channel + condvar` with atomics + per-slot scalars\nremoves the per-send Sender clone + per-message linked-list node\nallocations the channel does. Across thousands of fork-joins per token\nthat's the tail of the small-object churn that was bypassing the pool.\n\nThe 690 MB matches the no-leak arithmetic exactly: 640 MB Q4_K_M\nmmap-resident + 32 MB KV (f32, 16 layers × 8 KV heads × 2048 ctx ×\n64 head_dim × 2 × 4) + 20 MB JIT/workspace.\n\n\nAt 77% of llama.cpp CPU (~67 vs 87 tok/s sustained), the next 1.3x\ngap likely lives in:\n- Inner kernel still has 1.5-2x room (per Investigation A: per-worker\n  work 70.9 us vs llama.cpp's implied 30-50 us)\n- Worker load balance (workers using 3x of ideal 6x parallelism)\n- Reduced per-matmul fork-joins (compute-graph batching à la\n  llama.cpp's ggml_graph_compute)\n\nMetal/GPU path (147 tok/s at -ngl 99) is a separate multi-session\ninvestment.\n\n\n- `RAYZOR_LEGACY_POOL=1` runtime fallback to condvar+mpsc\n- `git reset --hard llamacpp-bench-baseline` reverts the whole session\n- 128/128 haxe regression preserved; Paris MATCH preserved\n\n\nI (the assistant) initially observed the 80-token bench showing\nvariance (4.6-28.4 tok/s on 5 runs) and decided to revert. The user\ncorrectly pushed back with their 808-token measurement showing the\nclean +33% win. The 80-token regime was too short to expose the steady-\nstate win and too sensitive to background contention.\n\nLesson for future fork-join tuning: bench on workloads matching the\nactual production pattern. 80 tokens is not \"real LLM workload\"; 800+\nsustained tokens is. The kernel-only microbench in /tmp/q4km_paired_bench\nwas load-bearing for c5ab136; the corresponding test for fork-join\nwork is \"sustained decode of 500+ tokens with prompt that doesn't EOS\nearly\".\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_spin_wait_worker_pool` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}